diff --git a/docs/layering.md b/docs/layering.md
index fc357ce..932099d 100644
--- a/docs/layering.md
+++ b/docs/layering.md
@@ -42,21 +42,6 @@ REPOS="mysoc myboard mesa-symbian tls"            # the repositories it is made
 RELEASE=recipes/release.sh                        # makes the release files into $OUT (CI and `phe image release`)
 ```
 
-A board can also carry **packages** preinstalled, besides the repositories it builds:
-
-```sh
-PACKAGES="webkit-symbian"                         # packages published by their own repositories' releases
-```
-
-A package repository (`package.conf`, the pdk's `package.yml`) builds and releases SIS files by itself. The board does not
-build it again: `board.lock` pins it by the commit of one of its releases, and `phe image packages` (the board's `RELEASE`
-recipe calls it before `phe image zdata`) takes the SIS of the board's architecture from that release, checked against the
-release's `SHA256SUMS`. The package's files go into the ROM as data files, with a **ROM stub SIS**
-(`z:\system\install\<package>_stub.sis`) that registers the package at the release's version and lists those files as
-eclipsable. That is the standard way to ship an upgradable component in a ROM: a newer release of the package, a SIS built
-with the ROM-upgrade flag (`make_sis.py --rom-upgrade`), installs over the ROM files, and removing it brings them back.
-The package stays a separate component, so its own licence terms (relinking an LGPL part, for example) are kept.
-
 `phe` finds boards by looking for a `board.conf` in each checkout next to core, so a new board needs no change
 anywhere else. `board.lock`, next to it, pins the commit of each repository a release is built from
 (`tools/board_lock.sh <board repository>` in the pdk).
